{"name":"Italian Basil","hex":"#5f6957","fetched":"2026-10-12","looked":{"definitions":[{"term":"basil","whole":false,"parts":[{"part":"Noun","definitions":["A plant (Ocimum basilicum).","The leaves of this plant used as a herb."]},{"part":"Noun","definitions":["The angle to which a joiner's tool is ground away."]}],"link":"https://en.wiktionary.org/wiki/basil"}],"entries":[{"title":"Genovese basil","blocks":[{"text":"Genovese basil, also known as Italian basil, is a specific cultivar of basil originating in the Italian region of Liguria. It is widely used in Italian cuisine and considered the essential ingredient for authentic Genovese pesto, a traditional sauce historically produced in the Italian city of Genoa and the broader region. Genovese basil is a certified protected designation of origin (PDO) product of Liguria and the communes of Genoa, Imperia, Savona."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/a/ac/BasilikumGenovesergro%C3%9Fbl%C3%A4ttriger.jpg/330px-BasilikumGenovesergro%C3%9Fbl%C3%A4ttriger.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Genovese_basil","wikidata":"Q1061638"},{"title":"Basil","blocks":[{"text":"Basil, also called great basil, is a culinary herb of the family Lamiaceae (mints). It is a tender plant and is used in cuisines worldwide. In Western cuisine, the generic term \"basil\" refers to the variety also known as Genovese basil or sweet basil. Basil is native to tropical and subtopical Asia and Australasia from India and Sri Lanka east to southeastern China, Taiwan, and south through Southeast Asia, the Philippines, New Guinea, to northern Australia. In temperate climates basil is treated as an annual plant, but it can be grown as a short-lived perennial or biennial in warmer horticultural zones with tropical or Mediterranean climates."}],"image":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/97/Ocimum_basilicum_8zz.jpg/330px-Ocimum_basilicum_8zz.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=api&utm_content=thumbnail","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Basil","wikidata":"Q38859"}],"images":[{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Generated_Image_December_27%2C_2025_-_4_02PM.png/500px-Generated_Image_December_27%2C_2025_-_4_02PM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/8/84/Generated_Image_December_27%2C_2025_-_4_02PM.png/500px-Generated_Image_December_27%2C_2025_-_4_02PM.png?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Production area of Genovese basil: Genoa Pra', Piana di Albenga, Diano Marina, and Sarzana","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3AGenerated_Image_December_27%2C_2025_-_4_02PM.png"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Basilico_Genovese_di_Pr%C3%A0-5.jpg/500px-Basilico_Genovese_di_Pr%C3%A0-5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/9/90/Basilico_Genovese_di_Pr%C3%A0-5.jpg/500px-Basilico_Genovese_di_Pr%C3%A0-5.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail","alt":"Two bunches of original basil from Pra'","link":"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/File%3ABasilico_Genovese_di_Pr%C3%A0-5.jpg"},{"srcset":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Bouquet_di_Basilico_Genovese_DOP.jpg/500px-Bouquet_di_Basilico_Genovese_DOP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 1x, https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Bouquet_di_Basilico_Genovese_DOP.jpg/1280px-Bouquet_di_Basilico_Genovese_DOP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.org&utm_campaign=parser&utm_content=thumbnail 2x","src":"https://thumb.wikimedia.org/wikipedia/commons/thumb/2/20/Bouquet_di_Basilico_Genovese_DOP.jpg/500px-Bouquet_di_Basilico_Genovese_DOP.jpg?utm_source=en.wikipedia.or